# `inserter.Instrument` Method

```go
func (i *inserter[N]) Instrument(
	inst Instrument,
	allowedKeys []attribute.Key,
	readerAggregation Aggregation,
) ([]aggregate.Measure[N], error) {
	var (
		matched  bool
		measures []aggregate.Measure[N]
	)

	var err error
	seen := make(map[uint64]struct{})
	for _, v := range i.pipeline.views {
		stream, match := v(inst)
		if !match {
			continue
		}
		matched = true
		in, id, e := i.cachedAggregator(inst.Scope, inst.Kind, stream, readerAggregation)
		if e != nil {
			err = errors.Join(err, e)
		}
		if in == nil { // Drop aggregation.
			continue
		}
		if _, ok := seen[id]; ok {
			continue
		}
		seen[id] = struct{}{}
		measures = append(measures, in)
	}

	if err != nil {
		err = errors.Join(errCreatingAggregators, err)
	}

	if matched {
		return measures, err
	}

	stream := Stream{
		Name:        inst.Name,
		Description: inst.Description,
		Unit:        inst.Unit,
	}
	if allowedKeys != nil {
		stream.AttributeFilter = attribute.NewAllowKeysFilter(allowedKeys...)
	}
	in, _, e := i.cachedAggregator(inst.Scope, inst.Kind, stream, readerAggregation)
	if e != nil {
		if err == nil {
			err = errCreatingAggregators
		}
		err = errors.Join(err, e)
	}
	if in != nil {
		measures = append(measures, in)
	}
	return measures, err
}
```

## How `allowedKeys` Is Used

The `allowedKeys` parameter is only applied in the **implicit default view branch** — reached when no explicit view matched. `NewAllowKeysFilter` is applied to `stream.AttributeFilter` only when `allowedKeys != nil`.

The code's own comments explain the nil semantics: a nil value indicates that `"the WithDefaultAttributes option was not passed, and all keys are allowed"`, while an empty non-nil slice means the option was passed and `"no keys are allowed"`.

Note that in the view-matching loop, `allowedKeys` is *not* referenced — the filter comes from the view's returned `stream` instead.

# `resolver.Aggregators` Method

```go
func (r resolver[N]) Aggregators(id Instrument, allowedKeys []attribute.Key) ([]aggregate.Measure[N], error) {
	var measures []aggregate.Measure[N]

	var err error
	for _, i := range r.inserters {
		in, e := i.Instrument(id, allowedKeys, i.readerDefaultAggregation(id.Kind))
		if e != nil {
			err = errors.Join(err, e)
		}
		measures = append(measures, in...)
	}
	return measures, err
}
```

# `resolver.HistogramAggregators` Method

```go
func (r resolver[N]) HistogramAggregators(
	id Instrument,
	allowedKeys []attribute.Key,
	boundaries []float64,
) ([]aggregate.Measure[N], error) {
	var measures []aggregate.Measure[N]

	var err error
	for _, i := range r.inserters {
		agg := i.readerDefaultAggregation(id.Kind)
		if histAgg, ok := agg.(AggregationExplicitBucketHistogram); ok && len(boundaries) > 0 {
			histAgg.Boundaries = boundaries
			agg = histAgg
		}
		in, e := i.Instrument(id, allowedKeys, agg)
		if e != nil {
			err = errors.Join(err, e)
		}
		measures = append(measures, in...)
	}
	return measures, err
}
```

## Summary

Both resolver methods pass `allowedKeys` straight through to each inserter's `Instrument` call. `HistogramAggregators` additionally overrides the histogram boundaries when a non-empty `boundaries` slice is supplied, per its documentation that instrument-provided boundaries `"take precedence over boundaries provided by the reader."`

